// The derivative reading: `(e) ~> grad(w)` evaluates its body exactly as the
// body evaluates on its own, and lets the extent ask how anything it computes
// changes with the seed.
//
// `d(v)` is ∂v/∂w — the partial derivative, every other free variable of
// the extent held fixed. A training step reads as the calculus it is:
//
//   (w - rate * d(loss(w, xs))) ~> grad(w)
//
// The medium derives the derivative program from the chain's own graph and
// emits it beside the forward one: no tape records the run, no operator is
// overloaded, and no second copy of the chain exists to fall out of step.
// The extent is what is differentiated. State that lives past it — a `<~`
// line, a handler's state — enters held fixed and is differentiated from
// the moment the reading opens, so where the install stands in the source
// is where the derivative starts.
//
// A `d` outside every reading leaves `Derivative` unhandled at the root, so
// the effect row is the proof that every question has a reading.
effect Derivative {
  d(v: Float) -> t
}

// The reading's install. Installing `grad` builds no record and pushes no
// world: the projection replaces the install, so the arm is never called,
// and the reading answers every op of `Derivative` with its argument's
// tangent (the query rule in src/derive.mn) without reading the arm at all.
// What the arm's body fixes is the answer's TYPE — shaped like the seed.
// It says `resume(w)` rather than `resume(tangent_of(v, w))` because the
// pinned boot that compiles this library as part of the wheel registers no
// `tangent_of`; the arm states the meaning once the boot does.
handler grad(w) {
  d(v) => resume(w),
}
